= On a Heuristic Viewpoint: Light Quanta

#text(size: 8.5pt, fill: ink-soft, tracking: 0.12em)[
  ANNALEN DER PHYSIK · RECEIVED 17 MARCH 1905
]
#v(0.5em)

A profound formal difference separates our picture of matter from our picture
of light. Matter we count --- so many atoms, so many electrons --- while
Maxwell's theory @maxwell1865 spreads the energy of a beam continuously over
an ever-growing volume. Yet the law of the normal spectrum, which Planck
obtained only by parcelling energy into discrete elements @planck1901, hints
that the continuous picture fails precisely where light is born and absorbed.

I shall therefore entertain the heuristic view that monochromatic radiation
behaves, in its production and transformation, as if it consisted of mutually
independent quanta of energy. A quantum of frequency $nu$ carries

$ E = h nu $

where $h$ is Planck's constant. The wave theory keeps its dominion over
interference and diffraction --- phenomena of averages --- but the exchanges of
energy between light and matter are settled one quantum at a time.

== The photoelectric effect

Herr Lenard's observations @lenard1902 supply the sharpest test. Light falling
upon a clean metal surface liberates electrons _at once_, however feeble the
illumination; and the energy of the swiftest electron is set by the _colour_
of the light, never by its brightness. On the quantum view this is no riddle:
each electron is paid by a single quantum, and keeps what remains after
settling the work $phi$ owed to the metal,

$ E_"max" = h nu - phi $

Below the threshold frequency $nu_0 = phi / h$ no electron escapes, though the
lamp blaze all day; above it, brightness multiplies the _count_ of electrons
and adds nothing to the energy of any one. Light, in its dealings with matter,
arrives in grains.
